<h2 id="california-rules">California rules for Santa Ana side hustlers</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>State income tax:</strong> 1% to 12.3% (plus 1% over $1 million) on top of federal income tax and 15.3% self-employment tax on net profit.</li>
  <li><strong>AB5:</strong> California's ABC test decides whether you're a contractor or an employee. Ongoing work for one business that's central to what it does may legally require payroll. Many creative and professional services have exemptions.</li>
  <li><strong>Prop 22:</strong> app-based drivers stay contractors but get an earnings floor for engaged time plus a per-mile payment.</li>
  <li><strong>Sidewalk vending:</strong> state law allows it, but Santa Ana requires a vending permit and health permits for food.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout-warn"><strong>Watch out:</strong> Orange County is expensive to drive in. A gig that looks like $25 an hour can drop below $15 after gas, tolls and wear. Always calculate your net rate.</div>

